gs-native-pc (patch 67): a vertex chunk larger than the ring can no longer write past it

ringAppend() wraps by resetting vertexRingOffset to 0 and then memcpy()ing the chunk. If the chunk
itself is larger than the whole ring, that reset happens and the copy runs past the end of the
persistent map: silent heap corruption, or a crash, in a path that only appears when one batch
exceeds 32 MB. Nothing measured reaches it today (the largest batch seen is 84 KB), which is exactly
why it would have stayed hidden until a scene produced one.

ringAppend() now returns kRingOverflow, without touching the ring, when the chunk cannot fit. The
draw path falls back to its own spillVbo: the ring is created with glBufferStorage, so it cannot be
respecified with glBufferData, and doing so would raise GL_INVALID_OPERATION. The shuffle path falls
back to the shuffleVbo it already owns for the non-ring case.

Also adds two counters to the GOW_GS_HW_STATS line, because ring pressure had no observable value:
  ringWraps   wraps of the ring in the window (> 2 per frame means the ring is too small)
  oversized   chunks that took the fallback (expected to stay 0)

Verified: builds clean (clang, C:\gow-build-jpn); 30 s Asia-disc run reports ringWraps=15,
oversized=0 at 0.5x, i.e. the ring is correctly sized and the new branch is not taken. No behaviour
change on the normal path.

